WEBVTT

00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:01.652
Venice doesn't float.

00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:03.789
It stands on wooden piles buried in

00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:04.648
the mud.

00:00:04.658 --> 00:00:08.029
Builders drove tree trunks,
mostly oak, larch

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.104
and alder, down into the clay under

00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:11.091
the lagoon.

00:00:11.101 --> 00:00:13.499
Under the Rialto Bridge alone, there are

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:15.717
about fourteen thousand of them.

00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:18.303
And ten thousand oak piles hold up

00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:19.929
St Mark's Basilica.

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:22.648
On top went wooden platforms, then brick

00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:26.042
and Istrian stone,
which resists salt water.

00:00:26.052 --> 00:00:28.167
So why has the wood lasted?

00:00:28.177 --> 00:00:30.684
The mud has very little oxygen.

00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:33.281
And without much oxygen, the fungi that

00:00:33.291 --> 00:00:35.716
break wood down struggle to grow.

00:00:35.726 --> 00:00:37.587
That's a big part of how buried

00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:40.212
trees have held up stone palaces for

00:00:40.222 --> 00:00:41.460
centuries.
